# The data artifacts of metalcraft-agent and how they compose.
# Gridwillow blueprint — https://gridwillow.com
# Normative spec for the pack format: specs/AGENT_PACK_FORMAT.md

blueprint metalcraft_artifacts
  title    The Artifact Graph
  tagline  what a person authors, what ships in a zip, and what a running agent actually reads
  branch   master
  stamp    v0.30.0

stat Artifacts   = 6 authored · 1 installable
stat Data root   = $METALCRAFT_DATA_DIR or ~/.local/share/metalcraft-agent
stat Spec        = agent pack format, spec version 2

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# what a person authors
# ------------------------------------------------------------------

group authoring "Authored primitives"
  note none of these install on their own — an agent pack is the only unit that does

lib persona "Persona" x1.5
  is    a system prompt plus the exact tool grant that goes with it
  why >
    The only artifact that carries a prompt. tools names native tools by hand and
    integrations adopts every <id>_* HTTP tool an integration provides, so a persona
    can take a whole surface without enumerating it. resolved_tool_names is the union
    and is what the registry and the step guard are built from — never the raw tools
    field. The packs serde alias is still read, because personas written before the
    0.30 rename are sitting on people's pods.
  src   src/persona.rs
  fact  On disk    = personas/<slug>.json
  fact  Versioned  = optional semver, drives seed force-upgrade
  list  Fields     = name, description, tools, integrations, skills, version, system_prompt
  num   Seeded     = 16

lib skill "Skill"
  is    a markdown doc with YAML frontmatter, pulled in on demand
  why >
    Frontmatter carries one description: line; the body is methodology. A persona can
    attach skills up front, and any persona holding load_skill can pull one mid-turn —
    strip_frontmatter serves the body and nothing else. Skills hold no tools and no
    permissions, which is why they are the cheapest thing in the system to write.
  src   src/skill.rs src/tools/load_skill.rs
  fact  On disk = skills/<slug>.md
  num   Seeded  = 25

lib apitool "HTTP API tool"
  is    one JSON document describing one HTTP call, exposed as one model tool
  why >
    name, method, url, headers and a JSON-Schema parameters block become a
    callable tool with no Rust written. Header values reference secrets as $NAME, and
    body_mapping decides how flat arguments become a request body — params_nested
    exists so a tool can expose scalars a model provider will validate while still
    producing deep JSON. poll: true exempts a status-check tool from loop detection.
  src   src/tools/http_api.rs
  list  body_mapping = none, params, params_nested, template
  fact  On disk      = api_tools/<name>.json, or inside an integration

lib integration "Integration" x1.3
  is    a versioned bundle of tool definitions and the credentials they need
  why >
    A directory laid out like a small project: integration.json, api_tools/, and
    optional personas/, skills/, README.md. Since 0.30 it has no independent
    lifecycle — no install, no enable flag. Availability is decided by declaration:
    a persona or preset that names it gets its tools, and nothing else does. The
    README is operator documentation the agent can read aloud while setting one up.
  src   src/integrations.rs seed/integrations/
  fact  Manifest  = integration.json (metalcraft_packs::IntegrationManifest)
  list  Fields    = id, name, description, version, requires_env, tags
  num   Seeded    = 8

lib preset "Agent preset" x1.6
  is    the agent's identity — a default persona, a roster, and its declared needs
  why >
    A pure composition object: it stores no prompt and no tools, only which personas are
    in play and which skills and integrations they need. Roles split the roster three
    ways — default is what a new instance starts as, subagent is what sub_agent may
    delegate to, internal is reachable but never offered in a picker. An empty roster
    with a non-empty default_persona is the minimal valid preset.
  src   src/agent_preset.rs
  fact  On disk        = agent_presets/<slug>.json
  fact  Ambiguity      = two packs providing one slug is an error, never a shadow
  fact  model          = a capability floor, not a model name
  list  Roles          = default, subagent, internal
  num   Seeded         = 2

lib flow "Flow"
  is    a saved node graph the daemon can run on a schedule, as a named persona
  why >
    Nodes carry a persona, so a flow was the one place the containment rule did not
    reach — it could name any persona on the pod. Binding a flow to a preset closes that:
    it may only name the callable roster. Schedules live in the flow document; the
    instance a schedule fires as does not, because an instance id is pod-local and must
    never travel in a published flow.
  src   src/flows.rs src/flow_bindings.rs
  fact  On disk   = flows/<id>.json (metalcraft_flows::SavedFlow)
  fact  Install   = lands with every schedule disabled, always
  list  Schedules = manual, every_minutes, every_hours, cron

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# distribution
# ------------------------------------------------------------------

group distribution "Distribution"
  note the archive is verified whole, in memory, before one byte reaches disk

lib agentpack "Agent pack" x1.8
  is    the only installable unit — a zip carrying exactly one preset and everything it names
  why >
    Self-contained by construction: installing must succeed with no network, so a pack that
    declares a dependency it does not vendor is invalid and there is no thin variant.
    content_sha256 covers every file except the manifest, so the manifest can pin what it
    describes without hashing itself. requires_env and domains are derived from the
    vendored bytes and never trusted from the author — a manifest disagreeing with its own
    contents is a rejection, not a warning.
  src   src/agent_packs/ specs/AGENT_PACK_FORMAT.md
  fact  Archive    = <id>-<version>.agentpack, DEFLATE
  fact  Manifest   = agent_pack.json, manifest_version 2
  fact  Presets    = exactly one; zero and two are both rejected
  fact  Size cap   = 64 MiB decompressed, measured on bytes read
  list  Carries    = agent_presets/, personas/, skills/, integrations/, flows/, memories.jsonl
  link  Spec       = specs/AGENT_PACK_FORMAT.md

ext registry "Pack registry"
  is    any host serving the four-endpoint pack protocol — a protocol, not a place
  why >
    axoniac.com, packs.metalcraftai.com and a company's own host are interchangeable to a
    pod, on the crates.io alternative-registries model. An id present in more than one
    configured registry is an error unless qualified, because a silent first match is the
    supply-chain substitution attack. Redirects are never followed.
  list  Endpoints = /version, /manifest, /download, /search
  list  Trust     = first-party, verified-only, explicit
  env   PACKS_BASE_URL FLOWS_BASE_URL

svc installer "Installer" x1.4
  is    validates the archive entirely in memory, then writes in a fixed order
  why >
    Seventeen checks run and every failure is reported at once, because an author fixing one
    error per round-trip is the experience the rule exists to prevent. Upgrades write new
    files before removing withdrawn ones, so an in-flight turn never loses its personas
    mid-sentence. Store garbage collection runs after the new refs are recorded, and is
    skipped entirely if any installed pack's manifest is unreadable.
  src   src/agent_packs/bundle.rs
  list  Rejects = path traversal, bad hash, two presets, uncontained persona, unvendored integration
  num   Checks  = 17

store packsdir "agent_packs/"
  is    installed packs, one directory each, holding what they provide
  why >
    The pack's own files land here verbatim — its manifest, its preset, its personas and
    skills. Its integrations do not: those go to the content store, and this directory keeps
    only an integrations.json map of id to hash. That indirection is what lets two packs
    share one copy and two versions coexist.
  fact  Path  = <data>/agent_packs/<id>/
  fact  Index = <data>/agent_packs.json
  list  Holds = agent_pack.json, agent_presets/, personas/, skills/, integrations.json

store packstore "Integration store" x1.2
  is    content-addressed integrations, keyed by hash and refcounted
  why >
    Ten packs vendoring the same calendar integration means one stored copy. Because the key
    is the content and not the id, two different versions coexist without conflict — which
    deletes the "highest version wins" ambiguity entirely; a pack simply gets the bytes it
    was built against. An entry is removed only when no installed pack still references it.
  src   src/agent_packs/store.rs
  fact  Path  = <data>/integration_store/<sha256>/
  fact  Hash  = metalcraft_packs::canonical_sha256 over sorted (path, bytes)

store lock "metalcraft.lock"
  is    every installed artifact pinned to name, version, hash and origin
  why >
    Its purpose is reproducibility across pod rebuilds and clones: replaying the lock
    reinstalls identical versions verified by hash, rather than drifting to whatever is
    newest. Written as a side effect of install and uninstall, so it always reflects the
    current set. Reads are lock-free; writes serialize through an advisory file lock,
    because the daemon and the workshop API can both mutate it.
  src   src/lockfile.rs
  list  Sections = agent_packs, packs, flows

lib seed "Embedded seed"
  is    the default personas, skills, integrations and flow templates, compiled into the binary
  why >
    include_dir! embeds the whole seed/ tree, so a released binary is self-contained and
    needs no folder shipped beside it. Skills and flows are write-if-missing; personas are
    version-gated, so a bundled persona with a higher semver overwrites the installed copy
    while a user-authored one — which has no version — is never touched.
  src   src/seed.rs seed/

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# the pod
# ------------------------------------------------------------------

group pod "The pod's data dir"
  note user-local always wins; a pack can add to the pod but never overwrite what you wrote

store localdirs "User-local files" x1.2
  is    everything you authored on this pod, in flat directories by kind
  why >
    The same shapes packs ship, written by hand or by the workshop. A local file shadows a
    pack file of the same slug, which is the whole override story: to change a pack's
    persona, save one with its slug. Pack-owned items are read-only through the API, and the
    error names the owning pack rather than refusing blankly.
  fact  Path  = <data>/
  list  Dirs  = personas/, skills/, agent_presets/, api_tools/, flows/, flow_templates/, chats/

svc resolver "Layered resolver" x1.3
  is    resolves a slug to a file and reports which layer it came from
  why >
    One function decides visibility for every artifact kind: user-local first, then each
    installed agent pack, then any legacy integration. Listings deduplicate by file stem so a
    shadowed pack entry never appears twice. Each hit carries its origin, which is how the
    workshop knows to grey out Save on something it does not own.
  src   src/integrations.rs
  list  Layers = local, agent pack, integration
  list  Api    = resolve_file, resolve_or_explain, list_files_layered

store keys "keys.json"
  is    the scoped plaintext secret store the $NAME placeholders resolve against
  why >
    Global keys and per-channel keys live in one v2 document, so a channel's secrets get
    short clean names without transport prefixes. Lookup is store-first then environment,
    which lets a pasted key override an env default. METALCRAFT_TOKEN inverts that on
    purpose — the control plane injects it per pod, and a stale pasted key must never
    shadow a freshly injected one.
  src   src/key_store.rs
  fact  Path    = <data>/keys.json, schema v2
  fact  Storage = plaintext; OS file permissions are the protection
  list  Scopes  = global, channel(<id>)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# live
# ------------------------------------------------------------------

group live "Live agents"
  note an instance is not a conversation, and that distinction is the load-bearing one

svc instance "Agent instance" x1.7
  is    a live agent minted from a preset, owning memory and many conversations
  why >
    A gateway session resets its state on idle; if an instance were a single conversation an
    agent would forget you between text messages. Instead the reset ends a conversation and
    the instance carries on, which is what makes per-instance memory worth having. Its preset
    is immutable for its life — memory is seeded from that preset, so swapping it mid-life is
    incoherent. Switching agents means starting a new instance.
  src   src/agent_instance.rs
  fact  Path       = <data>/agent_instances/<id>/
  fact  Id         = inst_<uuid simple>
  fact  Orphaned   = keeps memory and chats against a frozen preset copy; never deleted
  list  Origins    = workshop, cli, gateway, flow
  list  Follows    = persona prompts, tools, skills, roster, seed memories
  list  Never      = learned memory, conversations, name, persistent

store chats "Conversations"
  is    one thread with one instance, stored where transcripts always were
  why >
    Adding instances did not add a second copy of the transcript. A chat record simply gained
    an instance_id, which leaves the entire turn path untouched — this is grouping and
    identity, not new storage. Existing chats are backfilled into instances on first boot.
  fact  Path = <data>/chats/<id>.json

store base "Memory base"
  is    a preset's seed memories, built once per version, immutable and shared
  why >
    Built at install from the pack's memories.jsonl and refcounted across every instance of
    that preset — twenty instances hold one copy of the vectors, on disk and in RAM. Records
    are Source::Seeded, marking "the author gave me this" as distinct from anything the
    agent learned. Precomputed vectors are reused only when both embed model and dimensions
    match; a mismatch rebuilds silently, because recall degrades but never fails.
  src   src/memory/
  fact  Path = <data>/memory/presets/<slug>@<version>/
  num   Cap  = 5,000

store delta "Memory delta"
  is    one instance's own memory — everything it learned, plus its tombstones
  why >
    Every write lands here. Editing a base memory materialises that one record into the delta
    and shadows it by id; forgetting one writes a tombstone that also lives here, which is
    precisely why a forgotten memory stays forgotten when the base is repointed at a new pack
    version. Instance creation is therefore O(1): a pointer and an empty delta.
  fact  Path  = <data>/memory/instances/<instance_id>/
  list  Holds = wal, snapshot, tombstones, vectors

svc runtime "Turn runtime" x1.5
  is    assembles the prompt, builds the tool registry, and runs one turn
  why >
    Every entry point — CLI, daemon chat, one-shot flow node — constructs an agent the same
    way. It resolves the persona through the layered resolver, splices in the attached skills
    and the live memory profile, and builds the tool registry from resolved_tool_names so
    the step guard and the model agree on exactly one list.
  src   src/runtime.rs src/context.rs
  list  Steps = load context, resolve persona, assemble prompt, register tools, compact, execute

svc subagent "sub_agent"
  is    delegates a subtask to another persona, scoped to the preset's roster
  why >
    Its persona enum is the preset's callable roster and nothing wider, so delegation cannot
    reach an agent the pack never declared. The child inherits the parent's instance id, so a
    delegated subtask remembers into the same place rather than opening a second store nobody
    reads.
  src   src/tools/sub_agent.rs

job daemon "Daemon"
  is    the poll loop that fires due flows and scheduled follow-ups
  why >
    Each tick it evaluates every enabled flow's schedules, fires anything due, and drains
    persisted follow-ups. A single invalid schedule fails its whole flow rather than firing a
    guess, so the log makes the misconfiguration obvious. It also hosts the workshop API.
  src   src/daemon.rs src/scheduled_tasks.rs

entry workshop "Workshop API"
  is    the REST and SSE surface the GUI and the gateway both come in through
  why >
    One Axum router covers personas, skills, presets, instances, chats, flows, packs and keys,
    plus live chat and inbound webhooks. It is the same CRUD the *_meta tools use, so the
    prompt-driven path and the GUI read and write identically — there is no second code path
    that can drift.
  src   src/workshop_api.rs
  link  Schema = openapi/openapi.json

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# how they compose
# ------------------------------------------------------------------

preset -read-> persona "the roster"
  carry default_persona + personas[] { slug, role, description }
  why >
    The preset names slugs; the persona files are resolved separately and may come from a
    different layer entirely. A roster persona that does not exist fails the install, naming it.
  vol   0.9

preset -read-> skill "declared skills"
  carry skills[] -> skills/<slug>.md
  why   Validated at install alongside every skill the roster personas load, not just these.
  vol   0.5

preset -read-> integration "declared integrations"
  carry integrations[] -> integration ids, alias integration_packs
  why >
    This list is the containment boundary. Every persona's own integrations must be a subset
    of it, which is what makes the consent summary complete.
  vol   0.6

persona -read-> skill "attached skills"
  carry skills[] spliced into the system prompt at build time
  why   Attached up front, unlike load_skill, which pulls a body mid-turn on the model's call.
  vol   0.5

persona -read-> integration "adopted surfaces"
  carry integrations[] -> every <id>_* tool name, via resolved_tool_names
  why >
    The reason a persona can adopt a whole integration in one line. Native-tool integrations
    contribute here too, but only while actually installed.
  vol   0.8

integration -spawn-> apitool "tool definitions"
  carry api_tools/<name>.json -> HttpApiToolConfig -> one registered tool
  why   The tool's own name field wins over its filename, so a tool that lies still cannot hide.
  vol   0.7

agentpack -data-> installer "the archive"
  carry agent_pack.json, agent_presets/, personas/, skills/, integrations/, flows/
  why >
    Read and verified whole, in memory. Nothing is written until all seventeen checks pass,
    because a half-installed identity is worse than a refused one.
  vol   0.9

registry -data-> installer "download"
  carry GET /api/v1/agent-packs/{id}/download -> application/zip
  why   Content-addressed and immutable, so the ETag is the content hash and caching is trivial.
  vol   0.4

installer -write-> packsdir "what the pack provides"
  carry agent_pack.json + agent_presets/<slug>.json + personas/ + skills/
  why   New files land before withdrawn ones are removed, so every path that resolved still does.
  vol   0.8

installer -write-> packstore "vendored integrations"
  carry integration_store/<sha256>/{integration.json, api_tools/}
  why   Idempotent — an entry that already exists is left alone, which makes the second vendoring free.
  vol   0.7

installer -write-> base "seed memories"
  carry memories.jsonl -> memory/presets/<slug>@<version>/
  why   A corrupt line costs one memory and is counted, never the whole pack.
  vol   0.5

installer -write-> lock "the pin"
  carry LockEntry { name, version, content_sha256, source }
  why   Written on install and uninstall both, so the file is the current set rather than a history.
  vol   0.3

packsdir -read-> packstore "hash indirection"
  carry agent_packs/<id>/integrations.json -> { "metalcraft-calendar": "<sha256>" }
  why >
    The one hop that makes deduplication and version coexistence work. Ids are ambiguous
    across versions; hashes are not.
  vol   0.6

seed -spawn-> localdirs "first run"
  carry include_dir!("seed/") -> <data>/{personas, skills, api_tools, flow_templates, integrations}
  why   Personas upgrade by semver; everything else is write-if-missing and never clobbers your edits.
  vol   0.4

localdirs -data-> resolver "layer 1"
  carry <data>/personas/<slug>.json — checked first, always wins
  why >
    Checked on every lookup, before any pack is consulted. A user file shadows a pack file of
    the same slug, which is the whole override story — to change a pack's persona, save one
    under its name. The shadowing is silent by design; nothing tells you it happened.
  vol   0.8

packsdir -data-> resolver "layer 2"
  carry agent_packs/<id>/{personas, skills, agent_presets}/<slug>.*
  why >
    Walked in installed order once the local dir misses. For personas and skills a second
    provider is simply shadowed, but a preset slug offered by two packs is an error naming
    both — talking to an agent you did not install is worse than a failed lookup.
  vol   0.7

packstore -data-> resolver "tool layer"
  carry integration_store/<sha256>/api_tools/*.json
  why >
    Reached through each pack's integrations.json rather than by id, so the tools a persona
    adopts are the exact bytes its pack was built against. An integration nothing declares
    contributes nothing, because availability is by declaration and not by a global flag.
  vol   0.6

resolver -data-> runtime "resolved artifacts"
  carry (PathBuf, IntegrationOrigin) for the persona, its skills, and its tools
  why >
    The origin travels with the file. That is how a listing can mark a pack-provided item
    read-only without a second lookup, and how a miss can name the pack that would have
    provided it.
  vol   0.9

preset -spawn-> instance "minting an agent"
  carry AgentInstance::new(preset, origin) -> inst_<uuid>
  why   The preset slug is recorded and frozen; the version alongside it is diagnostics only.
  vol   0.8

instance -write-> chats "conversations"
  carry chats/<id>.json gains instance_id
  why   One instance, many threads. Ending a thread on idle does not end the agent.
  vol   0.7

instance -read-> base "shared recall"
  carry memory/presets/<slug>@<version>/ — read-only, refcounted
  why   Repointed to the new version on update; new records appear, tombstoned ones stay gone.
  vol   0.6

instance -write-> delta "learned memory"
  carry wal append -> snapshot + tombstones, fused with the base at recall
  why   Never touched by an update. Somebody's agent is in there.
  vol   0.8

runtime -read-> instance "identity for the turn"
  carry instance.persona, instance_id, and the preset's callable roster
  why >
    Read once at the top of every turn. The instance decides which persona speaks and which
    memory namespace gets written; the roster decides how far delegation may reach. An
    orphaned instance resolves against its frozen preset copy here and keeps working.
  vol   0.9

runtime -read-> chats "history"
  carry Vec<Message> -> token estimate -> compaction when over budget
  why >
    Loaded per turn and measured before the model call. Over budget the transcript is
    compacted rather than truncated, and anything durable in that summary is rescued into
    memory as Source::Compaction — so compacting does not quietly cost the agent a fact.
  vol   0.7

runtime -call-> apitool "tool call"
  carry HttpApiTool::call(args) -> reqwest -> JSON back to the model
  why >
    Fires whenever the model names a tool the persona resolved to. A non-2xx becomes
    ToolCallFailed carrying the status, so the model sees the failure rather than an empty
    success. Repeated identical calls trip the loop guard unless the tool is marked poll.
  vol   0.8

runtime -spawn-> subagent "delegation"
  carry sub_agent { persona, task }, persona enum = preset.callable_personas()
  why >
    Fires when a turn delegates. The persona parameter is an enum of the roster and nothing
    wider, so delegation cannot reach an agent the pack never declared. The child inherits the
    parent's instance id, so what it learns lands in one store rather than a second one.
  vol   0.4

subagent -read-> persona "runs as"
  carry preset.allows_persona(slug) — internal roles included, never offered
  why >
    Resolved through the same layered lookup as any other persona, so a sub-agent picks up
    your local override if you wrote one. Internal-role personas resolve here but never appear
    in the enum, which is precisely what the internal role means.
  vol   0.4

runtime -read-> skill "load_skill"
  carry load_skill { skill } -> strip_frontmatter(body) into the transcript
  why >
    Fires mid-turn on the model's own call, unlike the persona's attached skills which sit in
    the prompt from the start. The parameter is an enum of what this pod actually has, so an
    invented skill name fails at the schema rather than reading an arbitrary file.
  vol   0.5

apitool -read-> keys "credentials"
  carry $METALCRAFT_TOKEN in headers -> key_store::lookup_scoped
  why >
    Every $NAME in a header is also what the pack format derives the consent summary from,
    because those are the references authors routinely leave out of requires_env.
  vol   0.7

daemon -call-> flow "due schedules"
  carry parse_schedules(SavedFlow) -> ScheduledTrigger { cron, timezone, persona, inputs }
  why   Validation runs first; an invalid schedule drops the whole flow rather than firing a guess.
  vol   0.6

flow -spawn-> instance "arming a schedule"
  carry flow_bindings::arm(flow, schedule_id) -> a persistent instance, one conversation per firing
  why >
    Scheduled work accumulates memory across runs, so a morning briefer can notice it said the
    same thing yesterday.
  vol   0.5

workshop -call-> runtime "live chat"
  carry POST /api/chat { message, chat_id } -> SSE token stream
  why >
    The GUI and the gateway both arrive here, and both go through the same TurnRunner the CLI
    uses. A gateway session that idles out ends the conversation and leaves the instance
    standing, which is the reason an agent does not forget you between text messages.
  vol   0.8

workshop -write-> localdirs "authoring"
  carry PUT /api/personas/<slug> — refused with the owning pack id when not user-local
  why >
    Writes only ever land in the user-local dir. Editing a pack-provided item means saving a
    local copy that shadows it, and the refusal names the owning pack so the answer is
    actionable. The same CRUD backs the meta tools, so there is no second path to drift.
  vol   0.6

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# narrative
# ------------------------------------------------------------------

tab what "What it does"
  h  Six things you write, one thing you ship
  p >
    A [[persona|persona]] is the only artifact holding a prompt. A [[skill|skill]] is
    methodology with no permissions attached. An [[integration|integration]] is tool
    definitions plus the credentials they need. An [[agent preset|preset]] composes those
    into an identity — a default persona, a roster, and a declared list of what they need —
    and stores nothing of its own.
  p >
    None of them install. The only installable unit is the [[agent pack|agentpack]], a zip
    carrying exactly one preset and every persona, skill and integration that preset names.
    Self-contained by construction: an install must succeed with no network at all.
  note Hover a block for what it is. Click one for its fields and every connection in and out.

tab resolution "How a slug becomes a file"
  h  Three layers, first hit wins
  p >
    Everything addressed by slug goes through one [[resolver|resolver]]: your own files
    first, then each installed pack, then any legacy integration. A local file shadows a
    pack file of the same slug, and that is the entire override story — to change a pack's
    persona, save one with its slug.
  p >
    One deliberate exception. A preset slug provided by two packs is an error naming both,
    never a silent pick, because talking to an agent you did not install is worse than a
    failed lookup.
  ---
  p >
    Each hit carries its origin, which is why the workshop can grey out Save on something
    you do not own, and why a miss can name the pack that would have provided it.
  code |
    resolve_file(&paths::personas_dir(), "personas", "amy.json")
      -> Some((PathBuf, IntegrationOrigin::Pack { id: "amy-kitchen" }))

tab live "What runs"
  h  An instance is not a conversation
  p >
    Picking a preset mints an [[agent instance|instance]] — a live agent that owns memory
    and many threads. A gateway session resets on idle; if an instance were one conversation
    the agent would forget you between text messages. Instead the reset ends a thread and
    the instance carries on.
  p >
    Its memory is two layers. The [[base|base]] is the preset's seed memories, built once
    per version and shared by every instance of it. The [[delta|delta]] is what this agent
    learned, and it is the only one anything writes to. Recall queries both and fuses;
    forgetting a base memory writes a {{tombstone}} into the delta, which is exactly why it
    stays forgotten when the base moves to a new pack version.
  note An instance's preset is immutable for its life. Switching agents starts a new one.

tab consent "What you are approving"
  h  Derived from the bytes, never from the description
  p >
    The domains an [[agent pack|agentpack]] can reach and the credentials it needs are
    computed from the integrations actually inside the archive — every $NAME in a header,
    every host in a URL, every non-GET method. A manifest that disagrees with its own
    contents is a rejection, not a warning.
  p >
    Two rules make that summary complete. Every persona's integrations must be a subset of
    its preset's, so a pack cannot reach anywhere it did not disclose. And {{mutating_tools}}
    is required, because a read-only agent is a materially smaller commitment than one that
    can write, and a surface that cannot say which it is has not obtained consent.
  note Install never arms a schedule. Flows land disabled and the report says how many.

term "tombstone" = a forget-marker stored in the instance delta, so it survives the shared base being replaced
term "mutating_tools" = the derived list of a pack's tools whose HTTP method is anything other than GET
